\f1\fs22 It was Sunday night, the sort of evening built for other people\'92s families and fast food commercials. Hayley sat alone at her kitchen table in an apartment that ran end to end on forensic order and mild neglect: three mugs drying in a rack, paperwork stacked on every flat surface that wasn\'92t already covered by precinct detritus, and an indoor plant that looked as if it would lose the will to live before she did. From the window, you could just about glimpse the Layer\'92s terminal glow bouncing off a mosaic of towers, but in here, the only light was the cheap glow of her tablet and a desk lamp scavenged from an evidence locker. The silence was the kind reserved for those who had long since put their social lives out to pasture.\
