\f1\fs22 From the bedroom came the rhythmic sound of claws methodically shredding fabric\'97Chainsaw, her scrawny devon rex, had captured something new to destroy. She\'92d named her for her ability to reduce furniture to expensive confetti, and tonight\'92s symphony suggested she\'92d found her spare pillows.\
Hayley ignored the destruction; after the day she\'92d had, a few hundred dollars in upholstery damage barely registered as a problem. She meticulously arranged files for several ongoing and recent cases, leaned back, and exhaled a breath tainted equally by indignation, resignation and hard-eyed determination. If Shakti was taking the Scarlet Shower Surgeon case, she\'92d take a handful of his and revisit them in kind. It would be a formidable caseload (she\'92d get new cases for sure) but she was committed to shadowing Shakti all the way.\
It was plain as paperwork: Shakti had left Chantara to rot in PAPER\'92s Truth Central, never bothering to check on him. It made Hayley suspect he was no better at handling the Jacobson disappearance.\
She began by querying Chantara\'92s record. The more she looked, the more the record didn\'92t look back; she\'92d never seen anyone so perfectly invisible.\
\'93How could he have no armpit data?\'94 Hayley muttered\'97swiping, scrolling, banging out passwords on condescending login screens, submitting to ever more invasive seven-factor obstacle courses: eyeball scan, sticky finger printer, random DNA flake collector. She winced at the latest: a \'93health and financial data blend,\'94 which mined her tablet for blood pressure, heart rate, gambling tendencies and even her taste for punting on alpaca racing.\
Nothing.\
Hayley adjusted her jacket, braced and finally filled a foam coffee cup for an authentication gate that required a urine sample. The machine rejected it for \'93caffeine toxicity.\'94\
There were only two possible explanations:\
Either Chantara lived nowhere, traveled nowhere, consumed no services (legal or illicit) spent nothing, earned nothing, ate nothing, drank nothing, never used a toilet, never had sex, was never healthy or sick, never spoke, sang, coughed, farted, bled, or even so much as picked his nose.\
Or: someone very powerful wanted Chantara invisible.\
Hayley\'92s only firm evidence was Chantara\'92s recent guest spot on 
\f2\i Curb Your Individualism
\f1\i0 . She prepared for triple-speed bingeing, bracing for another round of humiliating multi-factor logins (\'93Please lick the camera for saliva verification\'94). 
\f2\i C.Y.I
\f1\i0 , funded by PAPER, seemed an odd place to make a ghost famous. Why would anyone erase Chantara\'92s history only to project him on the flagship PR show? Shakti\'92s mother, maybe. Or maybe not: Hayley guessed someone else in PAPER was orchestrating the spectacle.\
TRAMPS had spies in PAPER, and vice versa. About the only thing Hayley knew for sure was that Shakti\'92s stupidity didn\'92t quite reach spy material.\
She streamed every Chantara sequence from 
\f2\i C.Y.I
\f1\i0 . at triple speed. Her conscious mind glazed over until something in her subconscious yanked her back. Eyebrows knitted, muscle memory took over: she halted the episode, rewound and scanned. Played through, then skipped to the next, sweeping for anomalies.\
In every single one, Chantara was tortured\'97bruised, bleeding, battered in episode one; but by episode two, every trace was gone. Each fresh episode started with Chantara in mint condition.\
Hayley froze the stream and zoomed on Chantara\'92s flawless face. \'93No make-up,\'94 she guessed. \'93All injuries staged.\'94 Was Chantara even real? It would explain everything\'97his vanishing act in every data swamp, his absence of breath analysis and biometric trace. \'93Maybe he never even breathed,\'94 Hayley muttered. \'93At least, not as Anthony Chantara.\'94\
If the digital trail was a dead end, she\'92d try the physical one.\
Where 
\f2\i was
\f1\i0  Chantara now? Shakti said the Elvi had busted him out of Truth Central, the most \'93secure\'94 facility in the land. But as far as Hayley was concerned, the Elvi were karaoke artists, not soldiers. The more she thought, the more the whole thing felt staged. Yet, for what purpose, she couldn\'92t guess.\
She could hardly trust the digital records. Perhaps the bombing of the Stickett Inn was fake, too.\
Hayley, as thorough as a tax audit, dove in: searching the TRAMPS database for Stickett Inn files. Her tablet rewarded her with a pop-up bordered by grinning clowns:
